In article <2sq9lq$njl@crl.crl.com> wdstarr@crl.com (William December Starr) writes:
>What I was trying to do was get your system administrator to evaluate
>your postings to see if hey violated any of the Acceptable Use Policies
>of Florida State University and to take appropriate action if they did.

	He pissed you off. You snitched to his parents.

	That doesn't make you a censor.

	It does make you an asshole.

	Really, hasn't this tiresome schoolyard fight gone on far too
long? Rec.pet.cats be damned, the point is that it's uncivilized to go
tattling about such trivia. All it accomplishes is to make you look
petty, malicious and spiteful.

	Over in soc.singles there's a group consensus that no personal
ads shall be posted there. As you might imagine, given the fact that the
group's name is "soc.singles", clueless newbies who fail to read the
group before posting do commit this heinous crime from time to time. A
policy has evolved: the offender is emailed the FAQ, and is also flamed
mercilessly and publically for his/her stupidity (some of the regulars
take turns at this). No one writes any admins (who wouldn't care,
anyway), but the number of personals posted seems to be successfully
limited by the tactic.

	The analogy may not be perfect, but if there's really a true
consensus (i.e., virtual unanimity, not a mere voting majority) I can't
see why the group should need to resort to such mean-spirited reprisals
in order to police its content. Keep the fight verbal, and put away the
sticks and stones.

	And gmitchel, if you're gonna continue to post, PLEASE do
something about the ^Ms on your lines!

